\details{
Supported lifecycle types are \code{applybuff}, \code{refreshbuff}, \code{removebuff},
\code{applybuffstack}, and \code{removebuffstack}, plus the corresponding debuff types.
A positive \code{remove*stack} count leaves the aura active; an explicit zero
closes it. CombatantInfo entries are treated as pull-time state regardless
of their event timestamp. Consequently, an aura present in the snapshot and
never removed has 100 percent fight uptime. If no matching snapshot was
supplied, a leading refresh or removal is inferred to mean that the aura was
already active at pull, and the result is flagged in
\code{initial_state_inferred}.

Supply the complete lifecycle for the fight: use \code{paginate = TRUE} and do
not narrow \code{start_time} or \code{end_time}. With a partial download, a removal at
the beginning of the slice can be mistaken for pull-time activity, and an
application whose removal is outside the slice can be extended to fight end.

CombatantInfo arrays are documented by Warcraft Logs as containing an
ability game ID, source actor, initial stacks, name, and icon. The event
payload itself is an opaque JSON scalar, so this helper accepts the current
documented names and common ID-encoded aliases. Fetch source-aware data with
the \code{\link[=wcl_events]{wcl_events()}} defaults \code{use_actor_ids = TRUE} and
\code{use_ability_ids = TRUE}. In a CombatantInfo event, the outer \code{sourceID} is
the player carrying the aura and becomes output \code{targetID}; the nested aura
\code{source} is its caster and becomes output \code{sourceID}. A snapshot-only aura is
retained with \code{aura_type = "unknown"}. If matching lifecycle events identify
both buff and debuff families, the kind remains unknown and a warning is
emitted.

See the Warcraft Logs
\href{https://www.warcraftlogs.com/scripting-api-docs/warcraft/interfaces/RpgLogs.CombatantInfoEvent.html}{CombatantInfoEvent documentation},
